==German==

===Etymology===
From a conflation of {{inh|de|gmh|anelich}}, {{m|gmh|enlich|t=alike; similar}} (from {{inh|de|goh|anagilih}}, from {{m|goh|ana}} + {{m|goh|gilih}}) and {{inh|de|gmh|einlich}}, {{m|gmh|enlich|t=uniform; consistent}}. Cognate with {{cog|dum|aenlijck}}, {{cog|enm|anlike}}, {{m|enm|anlyke}}, {{m|enm|anlich}}, {{m|enm|onlich}}, {{cog|non|álíkr}}. More at {{l|en|alike}}; {{l|en|only}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈɛːnlɪç/|[ˈʔɛːnlɪç]}} {{qualifier|standard; used naturally in western Germany and Switzerland}}
